feat(touch): add a bit-banged GPIO I2C transport next to QUPv3

The QUPv3/SE13 path is the one part of this driver that has never run on
hardware: it depends on the bootloader having clocked the block and selected
I2C, and nothing in this kernel can repair that if it did not.  The sibling
OnePlus 9 port (b1ackm4x/hackios-xnu-kernel, bsd/dev/lemonade_touch.c) drives
the same Synaptics family at the same 0x48 address by bit-banging I2C on the
TLMM pins, bypassing GENI entirely, and it works on hardware.

This adds that as a second byte transport under the existing TCM layer.
kebab_touch_bb=1 selects it; QUPv3 remains the default, so the two can be
compared with one boot argument and nothing above kt_i2c_write()/kt_i2c_read()
changes.  The bit-bang path never reads or writes an SE or wrapper register.

The pins are SDA 36 / SCL 37 / reset 38 / IRQ 39, already the pins named by
the device tree's qupv3_se13_i2c_active group and by the touch node's
reset-gpio/irq-gpio.  Before changing anything the driver reads and prints
TLMM_CTL and TLMM_IO for all four, including the current mux field; then it
sets SDA/SCL to mux 0 (plain GPIO), pull-up and output disabled.  mux 0 is the
GPIO function this driver already uses for the reset pin, while the device
tree's "qup13" is function 1.  Both lines are open drain: high means release,
low means drive 0; neither is ever driven high.  Each failed transaction
prints the phase and the two line levels, so a failure says where it died.

The transport is diagnostic and read/write-command only: it issues the same
TCM identify/info commands as the QUP path and never an update, erase or
calibration command.
